WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.824
Welcome to Module 1 of the learning path,

2
00:00:02.824 --> 00:00:07.112
The Zero Trust Assessment as
Evidence: evidence, not a verdict.

3
00:00:07.112 --> 00:00:12.067
Microsoft's Zero Trust Assessment is a
PowerShell module that tests a tenant against

4
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:16.812
hundreds of configuration items drawn
from Microsoft's configuration guidance.

5
00:00:16.812 --> 00:00:20.124
It produces a reading of the
configuration on one day.

6
00:00:20.124 --> 00:00:23.813
In this module you'll learn what
that reading can and can't tell you.

7
00:00:23.813 --> 00:00:26.969
Then, on a fictional export,
you'll see why the tool's

8
00:00:26.969 --> 00:00:30.587
six statuses have to be sorted
before anyone counts them.

9
00:00:30.587 --> 00:00:35.837
The path follows the ten steps of the Zero
Trust Assessment implementation guide;

10
00:00:35.837 --> 00:00:40.140
this module covers Steps 1 and 6,
and takes about forty minutes.

11
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.040
Why does this matter?

12
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.723
The Zero Trust Assessment tests a tenant
against hundreds of configuration

13
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:54.552
items from Microsoft's guidance for Entra,
Intune, Azure networking and Purview.

14
00:00:54.552 --> 00:00:58.131
Release three point one point
oh, the current release,

15
00:00:58.131 --> 00:01:01.790
holds three hundred sixty-four
tests in seven pillars.

16
00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:03.200
It's read-only, it's free,

17
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.665
and it's the assessment component
of Microsoft's Zero Trust Workshop.

18
00:01:07.665 --> 00:01:10.377
The report is accurate on the day it runs.

19
00:01:10.377 --> 00:01:13.533
What it doesn't do is tell
you what a skipped test means,

20
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:16.115
what to fix first, or
when a finding is closed.

21
00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:18.296
Those are decisions for named people,

22
00:01:18.296 --> 00:01:21.040
and the report is the
evidence they decide with.

23
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.180
That's the title of this
module: evidence, not a verdict.

24
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.721
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

25
00:01:31.721 --> 00:01:34.068
which takes about forty
minutes with the lab.

26
00:01:34.068 --> 00:01:38.430
You'll say what the Zero Trust Assessment
checks, and name the four things it isn't.

27
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.589
You'll explain why it
doesn't replace ScubaGear

28
00:01:41.589 --> 00:01:44.667
under CISA's directive B
O D twenty-five oh-one.

29
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:50.255
You'll read Microsoft's rubric: risk level,
user impact and implementation cost.

30
00:01:50.255 --> 00:01:53.207
And you'll name the six
statuses a test can end in,

31
00:01:53.207 --> 00:01:57.067
and sort a skip reason into the
program's normalized taxonomy.

32
00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:00.579
Before you start, read the
chapter on the two assessment

33
00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:04.243
tools in Zero Trust and Assessment;
Unit 1 reads it with you.

34
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:08.206
For the lab you need PowerShell
seven point one or later,

35
00:02:08.206 --> 00:02:11.567
and the kit's folder,
examples slash z t a results.

36
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:13.290
No tenant is needed.

37
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.852
Unit 1: what it checks, and what it isn't.

38
00:02:19.852 --> 00:02:24.649
Microsoft Learn says the assessment
tests hundreds of items from Microsoft's

39
00:02:24.649 --> 00:02:29.077
configuration guidance for Entra,
Intune, Azure networking and Purview.

40
00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:34.879
The tool's release three point one point
oh sorts its tests into seven pillars:

41
00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:40.590
identity, devices, network, data,
infrastructure, security operations, and A I.

42
00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.240
Now the four things it isn't.

43
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.243
It isn't a verdict: a finding is evidence
for a decision a named person makes,

44
00:02:47.243 --> 00:02:49.703
and a pass rate isn't a maturity stage.

45
00:02:49.703 --> 00:02:53.465
It isn't a substitute for
ScubaGear, which is the next slide.

46
00:02:53.465 --> 00:02:57.165
It isn't a supported product:
its read me calls it a community

47
00:02:57.165 --> 00:03:00.865
project maintained by Microsoft
employees, provided as is.

48
00:03:00.865 --> 00:03:05.066
And it isn't a detector: Learn's
operations guidance for Global Secure

49
00:03:05.066 --> 00:03:09.415
Access calls it a configuration-posture
check, to be paired with alerts.

50
00:03:09.415 --> 00:03:12.782
The program's decision record
writes each of these down,

51
00:03:12.782 --> 00:03:17.390
with the scope, the run account, the
cadence and the pinned tool version.

52
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.428
The second point deserves its own slide.

53
00:03:23.428 --> 00:03:28.108
CISA's directive B O D twenty-five
oh-one names CISA's SCuBA tools,

54
00:03:28.108 --> 00:03:31.402
and the Zero Trust Assessment
isn't one of them.

55
00:03:31.402 --> 00:03:33.873
The chapter on the two tools in Zero Trust

56
00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:36.490
and Assessment lays
out where they overlap.

57
00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:42.266
Only ScubaGear covers Exchange Online mail
flow, such as external forwarding, S P F,

58
00:03:42.266 --> 00:03:46.428
D-kim and D-mark; Teams meeting
and external-access policies;

59
00:03:46.428 --> 00:03:50.165
SharePoint and OneDrive
sharing; and Power Platform.

60
00:03:50.165 --> 00:03:55.512
Both cover Entra I D identity, such as
legacy authentication, M F A, Conditional

61
00:03:55.512 --> 00:03:57.518
Access and privileged roles,

62
00:03:57.518 --> 00:04:01.528
and Defender for Office three
sixty-five threat policies.

63
00:04:01.528 --> 00:04:05.233
Only the Zero Trust Assessment
covers Intune devices,

64
00:04:05.233 --> 00:04:10.503
Azure networking and Global Secure
Access, Purview, and security operations.

65
00:04:10.503 --> 00:04:12.964
So an agency that already runs the Zero

66
00:04:12.964 --> 00:04:16.040
Trust Assessment monthly
still runs ScubaGear.

67
00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.880
The first runs alongside the
second, never instead of it.

68
00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.442
Unit 2 reads the report.

69
00:04:25.442 --> 00:04:29.280
It opens on an overview tab,
then a tab for each pillar.

70
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.559
Each test shows its risk and its status,

71
00:04:31.559 --> 00:04:36.330
and selecting a test describes what was
tested and the recommended remediation.

72
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.968
Every test carries three
ratings from Microsoft's rubric,

73
00:04:39.968 --> 00:04:42.242
defined on Learn's terminology page.

74
00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:47.539
Risk level: high means potential
environment-wide exposure until it's mitigated;

75
00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:52.755
medium, moderate exposure; low, defense
in depth or an operational optimization.

76
00:04:52.755 --> 00:04:57.847
User impact: high means many non-privileged
users have to act or be told;

77
00:04:57.847 --> 00:05:00.393
low means only administrators act.

78
00:05:00.393 --> 00:05:05.214
And implementation cost: high is a
program needing ongoing commitment,

79
00:05:05.214 --> 00:05:07.830
medium a project, low a targeted action.

80
00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.468
The tool's own source adds two more
risk levels, critical and unranked.

81
00:05:12.468 --> 00:05:15.943
These ratings feed the weighting
you'll meet in Module 3.

82
00:05:15.943 --> 00:05:18.260
They aren't a priority on their own.

83
00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:26.148
Each test ends in one of six
statuses, from the tool's own source.

84
00:05:26.148 --> 00:05:28.548
Passed: the configuration matched.

85
00:05:28.548 --> 00:05:29.948
Failed: it didn't.

86
00:05:29.948 --> 00:05:34.609
Investigate: the tool needs a person
to look before it's a finding or not.

87
00:05:34.609 --> 00:05:39.650
Skipped: the test didn't run, and it
carries a reason, such as not licensed,

88
00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:43.735
not connected, not applicable,
not supported, or timed out.

89
00:05:43.735 --> 00:05:47.173
Planned: the test is still
under construction in the tool.

90
00:05:47.173 --> 00:05:49.947
And error: the test couldn't be evaluated.

91
00:05:49.947 --> 00:05:53.860
A pillar owner reads their tab
with the risk and status filters,

92
00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.223
and notes every investigate, skipped
and error result, with its reason.

93
00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:00.773
They also note every failed result whose

94
00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:03.398
feature may not exist
in the tenant's cloud.

95
00:06:03.398 --> 00:06:07.570
That last case matters for federal
tenants, and we'll come back to it.

96
00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:17.070
Unit 3 is the rule this whole path rests
on: a skipped test is not a failed control.

97
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:21.698
The program records every result
twice: the tool's value, unchanged,

98
00:06:21.698 --> 00:06:26.327
and one of seven normalized results,
from the taxonomy in Evidence,

99
00:06:26.327 --> 00:06:29.945
Not Verdicts, a chapter of
Zero Trust and Assessment.

100
00:06:29.945 --> 00:06:31.233
Passed is a pass.

101
00:06:31.233 --> 00:06:36.107
Failed is a finding, and so is
investigate, until a person reviews it.

102
00:06:36.107 --> 00:06:37.295
Planned is neither.

103
00:06:37.295 --> 00:06:40.907
A test skipped for a missing
license is a licensing decision.

104
00:06:40.907 --> 00:06:44.695
One skipped because a service wasn't
connected is a gap in the run.

105
00:06:44.695 --> 00:06:47.757
One skipped as not applicable
gets a recorded reason.

106
00:06:47.757 --> 00:06:52.238
And an error, a timeout, or
any value the taxonomy doesn't

107
00:06:52.238 --> 00:06:56.720
list is unable to evaluate:
fix the run, or verify by hand.

108
00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.808
None of those skips is a failed
control, and none of them is a pass.

109
00:07:00.808 --> 00:07:04.270
Each one goes in its own
column, with its reason.

110
00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:08.745
Try it.

111
00:07:08.745 --> 00:07:11.825
Lab 1 runs on the kit's
fixtures, with no tenant:

112
00:07:11.825 --> 00:07:17.320
a fictional Contoso export in the shape
release three point one point oh writes.

113
00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.794
Step one reads the export's metadata,

114
00:07:19.794 --> 00:07:24.083
from the jay-son file the tool
writes under its zt-export folder.

115
00:07:24.083 --> 00:07:27.606
It's version three point one
point oh, tenant Contoso,

116
00:07:27.606 --> 00:07:31.620
thirty-three tests, and it ends
with the end-of-jay-son marker.

117
00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.308
That marker is the last thing the tool
writes, so a file without it is incomplete.

118
00:07:36.308 --> 00:07:40.841
Step two counts the tool's
statuses: one error, sixteen failed,

119
00:07:40.841 --> 00:07:45.194
one investigate, eight passed,
one planned, and six skipped.

120
00:07:45.194 --> 00:07:46.782
Now think about a pass rate.

121
00:07:46.782 --> 00:07:51.094
It would divide eight by a total that
moves with licensing and connections.

122
00:07:51.094 --> 00:07:54.334
Connect one more service next
month and the rate changes,

123
00:07:54.334 --> 00:07:56.195
though nothing in the tenant did.

124
00:07:56.195 --> 00:07:58.980
That's why the program doesn't report one.

125
00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.768
Step three lists what didn't
run, with its reason.

126
00:08:05.768 --> 00:08:08.442
One test is planned, under construction.

127
00:08:08.442 --> 00:08:13.932
The six skips carry five different reasons:
not licensed for workload I D, two not

128
00:08:13.932 --> 00:08:19.505
applicable, not connected to a service, a
timeout, and no compatible license found.

129
00:08:19.505 --> 00:08:24.705
Step four sorts those reasons with the
kit's own function, convert to Z T A result.

130
00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:27.343
Not licensed becomes skipped, licensing.

131
00:08:27.343 --> 00:08:30.180
Not connected becomes
skipped, disconnected.

132
00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.142
Not applicable stays
skipped, not applicable.

133
00:08:33.142 --> 00:08:35.842
The timeout becomes unable to evaluate.

134
00:08:35.842 --> 00:08:38.356
And an investigate result becomes fail.

135
00:08:38.356 --> 00:08:42.392
So that's four skips, four different
meanings, and none of them a failure.

136
00:08:42.392 --> 00:08:46.439
The investigate result is a
finding until someone reviews it,

137
00:08:46.439 --> 00:08:48.105
which you'll do in Module 3.

138
00:08:48.105 --> 00:08:52.780
The page lists every command for
the lab in order, ready to paste.

139
00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:57.555
Who decides?

140
00:08:57.555 --> 00:09:02.149
The decision record, from the guide's
Step 1, scopes each run: the tenant,

141
00:09:02.149 --> 00:09:07.468
the pillars, and the Azure subscriptions
for the infrastructure pillar; the run

142
00:09:07.468 --> 00:09:12.867
account; the cadence; the pinned tool version;
telemetry; and where the output goes.

143
00:09:12.867 --> 00:09:14.342
The see-so approves it.

144
00:09:14.342 --> 00:09:17.990
Then each pillar owner reads
their own tab, with the filters,

145
00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:21.340
and notes every investigate,
skipped and error result,

146
00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:25.806
and every failed result whose feature
may not exist in the tenant's cloud.

147
00:09:25.806 --> 00:09:32.018
For a G C C tenant, the workbook's baseline,
the tool uses the worldwide endpoints.

148
00:09:32.018 --> 00:09:35.643
In G C C High and D O D,
there's a case to know.

149
00:09:35.643 --> 00:09:39.211
Learn's Exchange Online service
description says anti-phishing

150
00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:43.505
impersonation protection and spoof
intelligence aren't yet available there.

151
00:09:43.505 --> 00:09:47.702
So a failed impersonation test in
those clouds is a boundary finding,

152
00:09:47.702 --> 00:09:50.560
not a configuration change to make today.

153
00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:55.697
The federal requirement.

154
00:09:55.697 --> 00:10:01.504
Scoping and reading the assessment supports
two controls in NIST special publication

155
00:10:01.504 --> 00:10:04.605
eight hundred fifty-three,
revision five: C A two,

156
00:10:04.605 --> 00:10:08.423
control assessments, and C A
seven, continuous monitoring.

157
00:10:08.423 --> 00:10:12.909
The approved decision record evidences
planning the assessment and its scope.

158
00:10:12.909 --> 00:10:15.185
Each pillar owner's dated reading notes

159
00:10:15.185 --> 00:10:17.873
evidence that the owners
reviewed the results.

160
00:10:17.873 --> 00:10:22.948
And the calendar entry, with its owner,
evidences the frequency of assessment.

161
00:10:22.948 --> 00:10:27.173
Each row also names a fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicator.

162
00:10:27.173 --> 00:10:30.423
Those indicators are written
for cloud service providers.

163
00:10:30.423 --> 00:10:35.273
The agency lines its own evidence up with
them; it doesn't report them itself.

164
00:10:35.273 --> 00:10:39.110
The evidence supports the
controls, and the assessor decides.

165
00:10:39.110 --> 00:10:42.511
So the evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module is the

166
00:10:42.511 --> 00:10:46.980
approved decision record and each
owner's dated reading notes.

167
00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.705
Check yourself with two questions.

168
00:10:52.705 --> 00:10:57.205
First: an agency already runs the
Zero Trust Assessment monthly.

169
00:10:57.205 --> 00:10:58.905
Can it stop running ScubaGear?

170
00:10:58.905 --> 00:11:00.080
The answer is b, no.

171
00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.785
CISA's directive names CISA's SCuBA tools,
and ScubaGear covers Exchange Online,

172
00:11:05.785 --> 00:11:09.058
Teams, SharePoint and
Power Platform policies

173
00:11:09.058 --> 00:11:11.743
the Zero Trust Assessment doesn't test.

174
00:11:11.743 --> 00:11:16.617
Second: a test is skipped with the
reason not licensed Entra I D P two.

175
00:11:16.617 --> 00:11:17.755
How is it counted?

176
00:11:17.755 --> 00:11:20.668
The answer is c: skipped, licensing.

177
00:11:20.668 --> 00:11:24.556
It's a licensing decision,
neither a failure nor a pass.

178
00:11:24.556 --> 00:11:29.840
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

179
00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:34.415
To sum up.

180
00:11:34.415 --> 00:11:39.028
You saw what the Zero Trust Assessment
checks, and the four things it isn't:

181
00:11:39.028 --> 00:11:43.565
a verdict, a substitute for ScubaGear,
a supported product, or a detector.

182
00:11:43.565 --> 00:11:46.728
You placed it alongside
ScubaGear, never instead.

183
00:11:46.728 --> 00:11:50.266
You read Microsoft's rubric
and the tool's six statuses.

184
00:11:50.266 --> 00:11:55.356
And in Lab 1 you sorted a fictional
export's skips into the program's taxonomy,

185
00:11:55.356 --> 00:11:58.265
and saw why none of them
is a failure or a pass.

186
00:11:58.265 --> 00:12:03.268
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module is the approved decision record,

187
00:12:03.268 --> 00:12:06.127
and each pillar owner's
dated reading notes.

188
00:12:06.127 --> 00:12:10.336
Next is Module 2, running the
assessment safely: the host,

189
00:12:10.336 --> 00:12:15.610
consent and roles, connecting in each
cloud, and the run and its hashes.
